==Original Issue==
Larger TTS models (Whether cloud-based or local, "large" relative to the phone) tend to have a large delay at the beginning of each paragraph. Certainly for the first paragraph, this is unavoidable, but for subsequent paragraphs, in order to prevent that gap, I'm wondering is there any way that we can throw those paragraphs out to the TTS model or to the service sooner so that there is less of a gap?

==Status Report: 2026-10-10 21:10 PDT==
NOT IMPLEMENTED — left as a plan. This ticket reads as an open research question
rather than a concrete spec, and the right answer depends on measurements you can
only take on-device, so I did not guess at an implementation. Analysis and a
proposed approach below.

What the code does today: `SpeechController.enqueueFrom()` queues *every* utterance
up-front by calling `engine.speak(...)` for the whole article (QUEUE_FLUSH for the
first, QUEUE_ADD for the rest). So from our side the text is already handed to the
engine as early as possible. The per-paragraph gap is therefore introduced *inside*
the engine/synthesizer (model warm-up / streaming synthesis latency per utterance),
not by us waiting.

The one API lever that exists is to pre-render audio asynchronously ahead of the
play head instead of relying on live streaming synthesis:

  TextToSpeech.synthesizeToFile(text, params, file, utteranceId)

Proposed approach (to consider):
  1. Keep a small "look-ahead" of, say, one paragraph. As soon as stream N starts,
     synthesize paragraph N+1 to a cache file via `synthesizeToFile` on a
     background thread.
  2. When N finishes, `engine.addSpeech(file, ...)` to play the pre-rendered N+1
     with zero synthesis latency, then immediately kick off N+2.
  3. Prune cached files as they are consumed.

Tradeoffs/risks to weigh before implementing:
  - Storage: one WAV/audio file per paragraph; needs cleanup and a cache dir
    (app-internal) plus handling of low-storage devices.
  - Some engines are streaming-first/cloud and may not support (or benefit from)
    `synthesizeToFile`; older APIs (pre-P/Q) had stricter file-format restrictions.
  - `synthesizeToFile` output encoding/quality can differ from `engine.speak`,
    so a voice could sound subtly different across the paragraph boundary.
  - It adds meaningful concurrency (background synthesis + callback ordering),
    which has to be integrated with the existing generation/currentIndex logic.

A simpler first experiment: verify whether the gap is really per-paragraph
synthesis latency. If so, the smallest change that helps is to *combine* adjacent
paragraphs into larger utterances (fewer `speak()` calls) where the 4 KB network/
synthesis boundary cost dominates, then layer on `synthesizeToFile` look-ahead only
if that's still insufficient.

Recommend leaving this open until you've had a chance to observe the gap on your
target engine; happy to implement the `synthesizeToFile` look-ahead when you
confirm you want that direction.
