What the symptoms point to:
- A 5–7 s pause at the *start of each paragraph*, plus occasional "delimiter-like"
  breaks after the first sentence of a paragraph, is the classic signature of a
  local neural TTS that (a) is not doing true streaming synthesis and (b) re-runs
  some model warm-up / encoder setup per `speak()` call. If the engine has to
  synthesize the whole first sentence/paragraph into a buffer before playback
  begins, you get a leading silence that scales with the length/difficulty of
  that chunk — exactly matching "gap at the start of each paragraph."
- The mid-paragraph "delimiter breaks" are consistent with the *sentence*
  chunking: if Wcast hands the engine one `speak()` per paragraph, some engines
  then internally split on sentence punctuation and pay that same non-streaming
  warm-up cost once per sentence boundary (hence an extra pause after sentence 1,
  not every sentence — likely the first sentence triggers a warm model path).
- Because Pocket TTS is local and "close to realtime but not quite," any fixed
  per-utterance overhead is very visible against a 5–7 s scale, whereas cloud
  engines hide similar latency behind their network/streaming pipeline.

Two cheap things worth testing before any code change (to isolate the cause):
1. Sentence-count probe — feed Pocket TTS a single `speak()` containing many
   sentences vs. one sentence per `speak()`. If the gap is proportional to the
   number of `speak()` *calls* rather than the amount of text, the cost is
   per-utterance warm-up (what Wcast already minimizes by queueing whole
   paragraphs). If the gap instead appears at each *internal* sentence boundary
   inside one long utterance, it's Pocket TTS's own chunking, and no amount of
   Wcast-side queueing fixes it — the only lever becomes pre-rendering via
   `synthesizeToFile` (the plan in the earlier status report) or asking Pocket
   TTS for a streaming mode.
