Your measurements settle it: the gap is (a) only at paragraph boundaries, (b)
proportional to paragraph size, and (c) always longer than any intra-paragraph
pause of any length. That is the textbook signature of *per-`speak()` synthesis
warm-up* in Pocket TTS — the engine re-runs its encoder/model setup for every
utterance, and the leading silence scales with how much it must synthesize before
the first audio frame is ready. It is not Wcast's queueing (we already hand the
engine every paragraph up front), and it is not Pocket TTS's internal sentence
chunking (those mid-paragraph breaks are short, and never 5–7 s).

So the fix is the one you suspected, and it is the `synthesizeToFile` look-ahead,
NOT paragraph-merge — and that matters precisely because of the tracking concern
you raised:

  • Paragraph-merge (join adjacent paragraphs into one `speak()`) would remove
    some gaps, but it *would* destroy per-paragraph tracking: several paragraphs
    become one utterance, so `activePosition`/the left margin would only move at
    the combined chunk's start, not per paragraph. You were right to worry.

  • `synthesizeToFile` look-ahead keeps each paragraph its own utterance. We still
    track and advance per paragraph exactly as today; we only *pre-render* the
    next paragraph's audio to a file on a background thread while the current one
    plays, then `addSpeech(file)` it the moment the current ends. Per-paragraph
    `activePosition`, skip, pause/resume (re-queue-from-index), and the left
    margin all keep working — the only thing that changes is the synthesis is done
    ahead of time instead of live.

Concrete plan for when you say go:
  1. Keep a one-paragraph look-ahead. On utterance N start, kick off
     `synthesizeToFile(paragraph N+1)` on a background thread into a cache dir
     (app-internal), keyed by utterance id.
  2. When N's onDone fires, `addSpeech(filepath, queueMode, params, id)` for the
     pre-rendered N+1, then immediately start pre-rendering N+2. Fall back to
     ordinary `speak()` if `synthesizeToFile` is unsupported or errors (so other
     engines are unaffected — this is purely an optimization, never a regression).
  3. Prune consumed files; clean the whole cache dir on teardown.

Risks to weigh before implementing (none fatal, all engine-agnostic-safe if we
keep the `speak()` fallback):
  • Storage: ~one WAV per paragraph transiently; needs a small cache dir + low-
    storage handling. (Can discard on the fly.)
  • `synthesizeToFile` output may sound subtly different from live `speak`
    (encoding/voice quality) — the one likely audible risk; worth a quick A/B on
    Pocket TTS before committing to it.
  • Concurrency complexity: background synthesis + callback ordering must slot
    into the existing generation/currentIndex logic without racing the pause/
    resume/skip paths.

This stays a research ticket for now; I have not changed code. When you're ready,
the smallest first step is a tiny probe (pre-render a single paragraph to file and
`addSpeech` it) to confirm Pocket TTS's `synthesizeToFile` output is acceptable,
then I build the look-ahead on top.
